    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "TheaterEvent",
      "id": "c0ea82dbcc512735",
      "title": "«Nina Simone - 4 οκτάβες Ελευθερίας»",
      "description": "",
      "fullDescription": "The stage at ARROYO Theater holds the weight of expectation. Nina Simone's music carries decades of history — civil rights anthems, jazz standards reimagined, the voice that contained multitudes. \"4 Oktaves Eleftherias\" — Four Octaves of Freedom — promises to honor that legacy. The lights dim.\n\nSimone was never just a singer. Classically trained pianist, activist, prophet, artist who refused every category. Her four-octave range was the least of her power; the real force came from what she did with those notes, how she bent songs into new shapes, how she made \"Mississippi Goddam\" and \"Feeling Good\" emerge from the same artistic vision.\n\nThe tribute format allows this complexity to surface. The production isn't simple recreation — it's exploration, an attempt to understand what Simone meant and means. The performers tonight carry the responsibility of representing not just a voice but a worldview, not just songs but a stance.\n\nARROYO Theater provides an intimate space for this kind of examination. The room holds audiences close enough to see every emotion cross the performer's face, close enough that the music becomes conversation rather than broadcast. When the band finds the groove of \"I Put a Spell on You,\" you're not watching from distance. You're inside the spell.\n\nThe audience for Nina Simone tribute programming has expectations shaped by deep familiarity. These are people who know the original recordings intimately, who carry \"Baltimore\" and \"Sinnerman\" as personal talismans. They've come to see whether tonight's interpretation can add to their understanding or will simply remind them of what they already know.\n\nIf you need Simone herself, no tribute can satisfy. Her presence was singular, unreproducible. But if you've been waiting for artists to grapple seriously with what she represented, to use her music as lens for examining questions of freedom that remain urgent — ARROYO Theater tonight becomes the room where that examination happens.\n\n| Info | Details |\n|------|---------|\n| **Date** | Friday, February 20, 2026 |\n| **Venue** | ARROYO Theater |\n| **Focus** | Nina Simone tribute |\n| **Title** | \"4 Oktaves Eleftherias\" (Four Octaves of Freedom) |\n| **Style** | Concert tribute with band |\n| **Good to know** | Intimate theater setting |\n\nNina Simone's legacy explored through performance: freedom in four octaves.",
